--- body ---
## Context

Current anchoring/provenance checks focus on presence/quality of sources, but an
additional lightweight signal may be informative: number of references cited.

## Question

Evaluate whether "reference count" should be added as a complementary anchoring
indicator (not a standalone quality score).

## Scope

- [x] Define candidate metrics:
      total references, unique references, references per table row,
      references per 1k output tokens.
- [x] Test sensitivity to citation inflation (spam references without grounding).
- [x] Evaluate relationship with existing provenance pass/fail outcomes.
- [x] Recommend whether/how to integrate in reporting.

## Exit criteria

- [x] One-page note with recommendation and caveats.
- [x] If retained, add explicit formula and anti-gaming guardrails. (Not retained — see below.)

Part of: 0465 — alternate anchoring score

## Findings

Analysis script: `src/aedist/analyze_ref_count_anchoring.py`
Per-run dataset: `report/inputs/generated/ref_count_anchoring_analysis.csv`

Data: 80 Exp2 runs across 4 agents × 2 arms × 5 reps.
Focused analysis on the optimised arm (arm3, n=40).

### Candidate metrics evaluated

| Metric | r with F1 (optimised arm, n=40) |
|--------|-------------------------------|
| n_bib_entries | 0.24 |
| bib_primary (primary-source bib entries) | 0.19 |
| bib_entries / n_rows | −0.18 |
| prov_presence (existing metric) | 0.07 |

### Key findings

1. **Weak correlation with quality.** Pearson r(bib_entries, F1) = 0.24 —
   no practical signal. All candidate metrics fall below |r| = 0.25.

2. **Top agents produce zero-bib runs routinely.** 14/40 optimised-arm
   runs have n_bib_entries = 0. OpenAI (F1 mean 0.67, best agent) produces
   5/10 zero-bib runs. The count reflects output *format*, not grounding.

3. **Citation inflation confirmed.** One Mistral run has 72 bibliography
   entries yet F1 = 0.257. High bib counts coexist with both high and
   low quality — the inflation scenario materialises in the data.

4. **Agent-specific format, not a generalizable signal.** Anthropic always
   produces a bibliography; OpenAI and Qwen do so inconsistently; Mistral
   produces large bibliographies in the optimised arm but zero in naive.

## Recommendation

**REJECT** reference count as a standalone or complementary anchoring score.

The metric confounds output format with grounding quality. An agent can
produce no bibliography and achieve high F1, or produce 72 entries with
poor F1. Anti-gaming guardrails cannot rescue a metric whose baseline
variance is dominated by agent style differences.

Retain `n_bib_entries` as diagnostic metadata in `exp2_mart run_summary`
(already present) — useful for profiling agent behavior, not for scoring.
